-- title:
--   The signal model, density generators $\Xi$, predictive priors $\mathcal P_0$, strategies $\Gamma$ and the maxmin objective (9)
-- statement:
--   A **signal model** is a measurable space $\Omega$ with a reference probability $P_0$, a filtration $\mathbb G=(\mathcal G_t)_{t\ge0}$ and a signal process $Z=(Z_t)_{t\ge0}$. For the volatility $\sigma$ it is **valid** if $Z_0=0$, every path of $Z$ is continuous, $Z$ is $\mathbb G$-adapted, $W=Z/\sigma$ is a $(\mathbb G,P_0)$-Brownian motion, and (Girsanov) every bounded progressively measurable $\eta$ yields a probability $Q$, equivalent to $P_0$ on every $\mathcal G_t$, under which $W^\eta_t=W_t+\int_0^t\eta_s\,ds$ is a $(\mathbb G,Q)$-Brownian motion.
--
--   Given the problem data:
--
--   1. a **density generator** $\eta\in\Xi$ (8) is a progressively measurable process with $\eta_t(\omega)=-\hat\theta^{\mu}_t(Z_t(\omega))/\sigma$ for some prior $\mu\in\mathcal M_0$ (depending on $(t,\omega)$);
--   2. the **predictive priors** $\mathcal P_0=\{P^\eta:\eta\in\Xi\}$ (2) are the probabilities $Q$, equivalent to $P_0$ on every $\mathcal G_t$, under which $W^\eta$ is a $(\mathbb G,Q)$-Brownian motion for some $\eta\in\Xi$;
--   3. the **strategies** $\Gamma$ are the $\mathbb G$-stopping times $\tau$ with $\sup_{Q\in\mathcal P_0}E_Q\tau<\infty$;
--   4. the **objective** of (9) is $J(\tau)=\inf_{Q\in\mathcal P_0}E_Q[X(\tau,Z_\tau)-c\tau]$; $\tau$ is **optimal** if $\tau\in\Gamma$ and $J(\tau')\le J(\tau)$ for all $\tau'\in\Gamma$; the **value** is $\sup_{\tau\in\Gamma}J(\tau)$;
--   5. the worst-case density generator (41): $-\eta^*_t=\hat\theta^{\mu^{\overline m_0}}_t(Z_t)/\sigma$ if $Z_t\le\tilde z_t$ and $\hat\theta^{\mu^{\underline m_0}}_t(Z_t)/\sigma$ if $Z_t>\tilde z_t$.
--
--   This is the continuous-time maxmin optimal stopping problem with learning that all results of the paper are about.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $P^\eta$ is characterized by Girsanov's conclusion rather than by its density (Mathlib has no Itô integral); the existence of $P^\eta$, which the paper asserts in §2, is a standing property of a valid model and holds on the canonical Wiener space with the natural filtration of $Z$. The filtration is any filtration with the stated properties, which includes the paper's filtration generated by $Z$. Membership $\eta_t(\omega)\in\Xi_t(\omega)$ is required everywhere, which yields the same priors as $dt\otimes dP_0$-a.e. membership. Stopping times take values in $[0,\infty]$; on $\{\tau=\infty\}$, null under every prior for $\tau\in\Gamma$, the payoff is evaluated at time $0$.
-- source:
--   Epstein & Ji, Optimal Learning under Robustness and Time-Consistency, arXiv:1708.01890v2, §2, pp.6–9, (1)–(3), (8); §3.1, (9), p.10; §5.1, (41), p.25

import Definitions.Def_EllsbergLearning_IsBrownianWrt
import Definitions.Def_EllsbergLearning_Params

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Set
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

noncomputable section

namespace EllsbergLearning

/-- The signal space of arXiv:1708.01890v2, §2, pp.6–9: a measurable space `Ω` with a reference
probability `P₀`, a filtration `𝔾 = {𝔾_t}_{t ≥ 0}` and the signal process `Z = (Z_t)`. -/
structure SignalModel (Ω : Type*) [mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω] where
  P₀ : Measure Ω
  𝔾 : Filtration ℝ≥0 mΩ
  Z : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ

/-- `Q` and `P` are equivalent on every `𝔾_t` (arXiv:1708.01890v2, §2, p.6: "equivalent to P0 on
each G_t"). -/
def LocallyEquivalent {Ω : Type*} {mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω} (𝔾 : Filtration ℝ≥0 mΩ)
    (Q P : Measure Ω) : Prop :=
  ∀ t, Q.trim (𝔾.le t) ≪ P.trim (𝔾.le t) ∧ P.trim (𝔾.le t) ≪ Q.trim (𝔾.le t)

/-- The process `W^η_t = Z_t/σ + ∫₀ᵗ η_s ds` of (1), arXiv:1708.01890v2, p.7, with `W = Z/σ`
(§2, Step 1–2, pp.8–9). It is applied only to bounded progressively measurable `η`, for which the
integral is a genuine Lebesgue integral of a bounded measurable function. -/
def Weta {Ω : Type*} (σ : ℝ) (Z η : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ :=
  fun t ω => Z t ω / σ + ∫ s in (0 : ℝ)..(t : ℝ), η s.toNNReal ω

namespace SignalModel

variable {Ω : Type*} [mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω]

/-- The standing assumptions on the signal space (arXiv:1708.01890v2, §2, pp.6–9), for the signal
volatility `σ`:

* `P₀` is a probability measure;
* `Z₀ = 0`, and every path `t ↦ Z_t(ω)` is continuous;
* `Z` is `𝔾`-adapted and `W = Z/σ` is a `(𝔾, P₀)`-Brownian motion (Step 2, p.9);
* (Girsanov, p.6–7) every bounded progressively measurable `η` defines a probability `P^η`
  equivalent to `P₀` on each `𝔾_t` under which `W^η = W + ∫₀^· η_s ds` is a `𝔾`-Brownian motion.

**Formalization Note.** The last clause is the paper's §2 assertion "each η ∈ Ξ defines a
probability measure on G∞, denoted P^η, that is equivalent to P0 on each Gt ... By the Girsanov
Theorem, dW^η_t = η_t dt + dW_t is a Brownian motion under P^η", stated as a property of the model
because Mathlib has no Itô integral or Girsanov theorem; it holds, for instance, on the canonical
space of continuous paths with Wiener measure and the natural filtration of `Z`, and the milestone
`worst_case_prior` asserts that a valid model exists. It is a real condition: on the filtration
augmented by all `P₀`-null sets of `𝔾_∞` the conclusion fails, since `P^η` is singular to `P₀` on
`𝔾_∞` (under it `Z_t/t` converges to `θ₀` or `θ₁`, under `P₀` to `0`), so the paper's filtration
has to be read with an augmentation that keeps its own p.6 assertion true. The filtration here is
any filtration with these properties. -/
structure IsValid (M : SignalModel Ω) (σ : ℝ) : Prop where
  isProb : IsProbabilityMeasure M.P₀
  Z_zero : ∀ ω, M.Z 0 ω = 0
  Z_cont : ∀ ω, Continuous fun t => M.Z t ω
  Z_adapted : ∀ t, StronglyMeasurable[M.𝔾 t] (M.Z t)
  bm : IsBrownianWrt M.𝔾 M.P₀ (fun t ω => M.Z t ω / σ)
  girsanov : ∀ η : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ, IsStronglyProgressive M.𝔾 η → (∃ K, ∀ t ω, |η t ω| ≤ K) →
    ∃ Q : Measure Ω, IsProbabilityMeasure Q ∧ LocallyEquivalent M.𝔾 Q M.P₀ ∧
      IsBrownianWrt M.𝔾 Q (Weta σ M.Z η)

variable (p : Params) (M : SignalModel Ω)

/-- `η` is a density generator in `Ξ` ((8), arXiv:1708.01890v2, p.9, with (10)–(12)): it is
progressively measurable and at every `(t, ω)` equals `−θ̂^µ_t/σ` for some prior
`µ = µ^m ∈ M₀`, `m ∈ [m̲₀, m̄₀]`, evaluated at the observed signal `Z_t(ω)`.

**Formalization Note.** The paper asks `η_t(ω) ∈ Ξ_t(ω)` for `dt ⊗ dP₀`-a.e. `(t, ω)`. Every such
`η` agrees `dt ⊗ dP₀`-a.e. with its clamp to the interval `Ξ_t(ω)`, which is progressively
measurable and lies in `Ξ_t(ω)` everywhere, and `P^η` depends only on the a.e. class of `η`; so
requiring membership everywhere defines the same set of priors. -/
def IsGenerator (η : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : Prop :=
  IsStronglyProgressive M.𝔾 η ∧
    ∀ t ω, ∃ m ∈ Icc p.mLo0 p.mUp0, η t ω = -p.thetaHat m t (M.Z t ω) / p.σ

/-- `Q` is the predictive prior `P^η` with density generator `η` (arXiv:1708.01890v2, §2,
pp.6–7): `η ∈ Ξ`, `Q` is a probability measure equivalent to `P₀` on every `𝔾_t`, and
`W^η = Z/σ + ∫₀^· η_s ds` is a `(𝔾, Q)`-Brownian motion.

**Formalization Note.** This is the Girsanov characterization of `P^η`; the density
`exp{−½∫η² ds − ∫η dW}` (printed on p.6 without the factor ½) cannot be written without an Itô
integral. For the filtration generated by `Z`, any `Q` with these properties equals `P^η` on every
`𝔾_t` (martingale representation), and the objective (9) depends on `Q` only through these
restrictions. -/
def HasGenerator (Q : Measure Ω) (η : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : Prop :=
  IsGenerator p M η ∧ IsProbabilityMeasure Q ∧ LocallyEquivalent M.𝔾 Q M.P₀ ∧
    IsBrownianWrt M.𝔾 Q (Weta p.σ M.Z η)

/-- The set `P0 = {P^η : η ∈ Ξ}` of predictive priors ((2), arXiv:1708.01890v2, p.7). -/
def priors : Set (Measure Ω) := {Q | ∃ η, HasGenerator p M Q η}

/-- The value of a stopping time as a real number. On `{τ = ∞}` it is `0`; this set is null for
every prior when `τ` is a stopping strategy (its expectation is finite), so the convention does
not affect the objective. -/
def stopTime (τ : Ω → WithTop ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : ℝ := (((τ ω).untopD 0 : ℝ≥0) : ℝ)

/-- The realized net payoff `X_τ − cτ` of stopping at `τ` (arXiv:1708.01890v2, (9), p.10), with
`X_τ = X(τ, Z_τ)` from (16). -/
def gain (τ : Ω → WithTop ℝ≥0) (ω : Ω) : ℝ :=
  p.X (stopTime τ ω) (M.Z ((τ ω).untopD 0) ω) - p.c * stopTime τ ω

/-- The set `Γ` of stopping strategies (arXiv:1708.01890v2, p.10): `𝔾`-stopping times `τ` that are
"uniformly integrable", `sup_{Q ∈ P0} E_Q τ < ∞`. -/
def strategies : Set (Ω → WithTop ℝ≥0) :=
  {τ | IsStoppingTime M.𝔾 τ ∧ (⨆ Q ∈ priors p M, ∫⁻ ω, (τ ω : ℝ≥0∞) ∂Q) < ⊤}

/-- The maxmin objective `min_{P ∈ P0} E_P(X_τ − cτ)` of (9), arXiv:1708.01890v2, p.10, as the
infimum over the predictive priors.

**Formalization Note.** For `τ ∈ Γ` and nonempty `P0` the integrand is integrable under every
prior (`X` is bounded and `E_Q τ < ∞`), and the family is bounded below by
`−c · sup_Q E_Q τ`. -/
def J (τ : Ω → WithTop ℝ≥0) : ℝ := ⨅ Q : priors p M, ∫ ω, gain p M τ ω ∂(Q : Measure Ω)

/-- `τ` is optimal in (9): `τ ∈ Γ` and `J(τ') ≤ J(τ)` for every `τ' ∈ Γ`. -/
def IsOptimal (τ : Ω → WithTop ℝ≥0) : Prop :=
  τ ∈ strategies p M ∧ ∀ τ' ∈ strategies p M, J p M τ' ≤ J p M τ

/-- The value `max_{τ ∈ Γ} min_{P ∈ P0} E_P(X_τ − cτ)` of (9), as the supremum of `J` over `Γ`. -/
def value : ℝ := sSup (J p M '' strategies p M)

/-- The density generator (41) of the worst-case prior `P*` (arXiv:1708.01890v2, p.25):
`−η_t = (θ̂^{µ̄}_t/σ)1_{Z_t ≤ z̃_t} + (θ̂^{µ̲}_t/σ)1_{Z_t > z̃_t}`, where `µ̄`, `µ̲` are the priors with
posteriors `m̄_t`, `m̲_t`. -/
def etaStar (zt : ℝ → ℝ) : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ := fun t ω =>
  if M.Z t ω ≤ zt t then -p.thetaHat p.mUp0 t (M.Z t ω) / p.σ
  else -p.thetaHat p.mLo0 t (M.Z t ω) / p.σ

end SignalModel

end EllsbergLearning


